    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "message": "Guidelines list lower back pain, change in urine volume, bone aches, and muscle cramps as presenting signs of TDF toxicity including Fanconi syndrome and bone mineral density loss. Patient reports persistent dull lower back pain and heavy feeling in both thighs worsening over 4 months, plus trace proteinuria and low serum phosphate (0.78 mmol/L). Proteinuria and hypophosphataemia are consistent with proximal tubular dysfunction. Occupational driving is an alternative explanation for back pain. However, the combination of back pain plus renal tubular signs warrants TDF toxicity workup.",
    "recommended_action": "Do LFTs and RFTs with eGFR, urine protein/creatinine ratio, and serum phosphate. If deranged, consider switching TDF to TAF or alternative per guidelines.",
    "severity": "high",
    "title": "TDF toxicity signs: back pain, proteinuria, hypophosphataemia"
  },
